# the PE image writer: an executable from the image record of a link
#
# every address, file offset and size of an output section comes from the
# layout record, and every byte from the relocated record. the writer builds
# the PE/COFF model the PE/COFF writer turns into bytes. the headers are the
# MS-DOS header and program every PE linker writes, the PE signature, the file
# header, the optional header with its sixteen data directories and a section
# header for each section of the image, which the header measure sizes from
# the laid out image. the optional header layout is the one whose address is
# as wide as the architecture's, the machine is the architecture's COFF code,
# the image base the link's, the section and file alignment the layout
# rules', the subsystem the target's or else the host's, and the system
# version the target's, else the host's, else the one link.exe states. the
# characteristics follow the layout and the output kind: an image the loader
# may place anywhere is dynamic base, and high entropy where its addresses
# are 64 bits wide. every executable is NX compatible and terminal server
# aware, as link.exe makes one. the checksum is PE Format's, of the bytes
# written.
#
# a section no segment loads, as the debugging information a program database
# would hold, is no section of the image and is left out, as are output
# sections that hold no byte. .reloc is a table the image reserves before
# layout, measured from the link's loader relocation sites where layout puts
# them, and laid out last in a segment of its own, as link.exe places it: one
# block per 4 KiB page, in address order, each padded to a multiple of four
# bytes. the writer fills it from the same sites at the addresses relocate
# filled in, which take the bytes layout left. an image the loader may place anywhere and that
# holds no address to relocate is refused, as link.exe refuses it.
#
# the inputs' function tables, .pdata, lie in one output section laid out and
# relocated as any other, each entry living with the function it names. the
# writer reads the relocated entries in the layout the machine's function
# table row states, sorts them by the address their function starts at, as
# the loader's binary search needs, and names them in the exception
# directory. an ARM64 entry's unwind word is kept as written, packed or the
# address of its .xdata. an image of two output sections of function tables,
# or of a machine with no function table layout, is refused.
#
# an image that imports holds, per DLL by rank, one import descriptor, its
# run of the import lookup table and of the import address table, each
# closed by an empty slot, a hint/name entry per import by name and the
# DLL's name, as link.exe lays them. the import address table is the slot
# table the link reserves, and the descriptors, the lookup table, the
# hint/name table and the DLL names are a table the image reserves ahead of
# it in .idata, measured from the imports. a slot of either table holds the
# address of its import's hint/name entry, or the ordinal flag and the
# ordinal for an import by ordinal, as its short import object's name type
# says. an import whose input states no hint takes hint zero, which the
# loader reads as none. the import and import address table directories name
# the descriptors and the address table. a call to an import goes through the
# import's thunk, which the link writes from its role's template.
#
# the image's thread-local template is .tls, which collects the .tls$ input
# sections in name order, and code reaches a variable through the index the
# loader writes in _tls_index and the variable's offset in .tls. the TLS
# directory names the template's start and end, the index cell, the callback
# array and the zero fill, and its characteristics state the template's
# alignment, as lld-link states it. a CRT supplies the directory as
# _tls_used, with _tls_index beside it and the callbacks between its .CRT$XLA
# and .CRT$XLZ bounds, and the image's TLS directory names that definition. a
# link that holds thread-local data and whose inputs define no _tls_used
# supplies them itself: the image reserves the directory in .rdata and the
# index cell in .data, _tls_index is a bound symbol in that cell, and the link
# makes the .CRT$XLA and .CRT$XLZ bounds, each a null pointer, so callbacks in
# .CRT$XL sections between them form a closed array, and a callback section
# that is not read-only data, as they are, would lie outside them and is
# refused. the directory's addresses are base relocation sites of an image
# the loader may place anywhere. a machine whose C symbols carry a prefix
# spells these names otherwise, which the link does not hold yet, so its
# thread-local data is refused. a link that needs what the image does not
# hold yet, a delay loaded import or an export, is refused naming what it
# needs.

